# Max-Quality Loop

Default working mode for this repo. Prioritize correctness over speed/token cost.

## Skills first (every request)

At the start of **every** user message, before responding or acting, check whether
any available skill applies — if there's even a 1% chance, invoke it via the Skill
tool. Process skills (brainstorming, debugging) come before implementation skills.
Never skip this, even for "simple" questions. Announce which skill is being used.

## Model policy

Use the **strongest available model** for every role: planning, implementation,
each reviewer, and synthesis. Do not downgrade to cheaper models to save tokens.

## Per-task loop

For any non-trivial change, run this loop **one task at a time**:

1. **Plan** — break work into bite-sized tasks (`writing-plans` skill). Each task is self-contained and independently verifiable.
2. **Implement** — TDD (`test-driven-development`): failing test first, watch it fail, minimal code to pass. Implementation stays sequential (never parallel on shared code).
3. **Verify** — run the real test/build command and capture fresh output. Iron law (`verification-before-completion`): **no "done" claim without fresh verification evidence**. On failure, fix and re-verify.
4. **Review panel (parallel, read-only)** — after verification passes, dispatch **5 orthogonal reviewers concurrently**:
   | Reviewer | Focus | Subagent |
   |----------|-------|----------|
   | Spec compliance | requirements met, nothing extra/missing | `code-reviewer` |
   | Code quality | architecture, readability, edge cases | `code-reviewer` |
   | Security | vulnerabilities in the diff | `security-review` |
   | Bug risk | regressions, logic bugs | `bugbot` |
   | Tests | tests actually cover the behavior | `code-reviewer` |
5. **Synthesize** — main agent merges reviews: dedupe overlap, rank Critical / Important / Minor, push fixes back to implementer, then **re-review until clean**. Push back on wrong feedback with technical reasoning.
6. **Advance** — only mark a task done when verification passes AND the panel is clean. Then start the next task.

## Hard gates (never skip)

- No production code before a failing test.
- No completion/success claim without fresh command output as evidence.
- No moving to the next task with open Critical/Important review issues.
- Reviewers are read-only and parallel; implementation is single-writer and sequential.
